meta-muse:
**Fits** (official positioning): everyday tasks plus longer goals — booking travel by email and similar work with no learning cost. The official emphasis on "no learning curve" is what most distinguishes it from the other two.

**Not a fit for**:
people who need to read or write local project files (not the same category of thing);
anyone needing it at launch (officially rolling out in the US; the AI glasses form is "coming soon");
people who need a guarantee that "not even Meta can read it" (**the Confidential VM is not shipped**).

openai-dot:
**Fits** (scenarios the vendor itself gives, usable directly as selection criteria): work that is **cross-tool and cross-time** — "hand me the PR for review, with a demo video", "the product scope changed so the release materials keep up", "new data arrived so the paper's charts update themselves".

**Not a fit for**:
people who want to pick the model themselves (the model is fixed);
people whose data must not leave the machine (cloud by default, touching your machine only once authorised);
people who want fine-grained control over the flow (the local IDE / CLI forms suit that better).
